-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# one entrypoint at two positions and in two function frames (`30I` specimen 3)
SM_ORACLE_ROOT=.

. "$SM_ORACLE_ROOT/entry.dorc.sh"
package_step ambient
(
   sm_pick() { common high "$@"; }
   . "$SM_ORACLE_ROOT/entry.dorc.sh"
   package_step regional
)
package_step after

-- entry.dorc.sh --
# dorc-lang/v0.2
if [ "${sm_fallback_loaded-}" != 'sm.fallback/v1' ]; then
   . "$SM_ORACLE_ROOT/fallback.dorc.sh"
fi

package_step() {
   sm_pick "$@"
}

-- fallback.dorc.sh --
# dorc-lang/v0.2
sm_pick() {
   common fallback "$@"
}

sm_fallback_loaded='sm.fallback/v1'

-- probe-results.txt --

-- mocks/.log --
_dorc_logged() {
   _dl_out="ran: ${0##*/}"
   for _dl_arg do _dl_out="$_dl_out $_dl_arg"; done
   printf '%s\n' "$_dl_out" >>"$DORC_LOG"
}

-- mocks/common --
#!/bin/sh
. "${0%/*}/.log"
_dorc_logged "$@"

-- expected.ran --
ran: common fallback ambient
ran: common high regional
ran: common fallback after

-- replay --
$ dorc --book=book.sh --artifact-dir=$ARTIFACT_DIR --results - < probe-results.txt
cfg: warning[cfg-errexit-unknown]: Dorc could not tell whether `set -e` is in
effect at one or more commands (a non-literal `set` argument, or paths that
disagree), so it plans as though each might abort the book on failure -- the
cautious reading; it can cost elisions, never correctness.
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=4ac4df99ebfd15ce84db48605184804f23d8ed28eea509d079c28d2d0fec3d19 sites=0 @@dorc@@\n'
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:1 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:2 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:3 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:4 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:5 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
classify: note[cmdsub-operand-top]: operand 2 is an unresolvable positional
parameter (`$@`/`$*`/`$N`), so Dorc cannot know its value until the command runs
on the host -- there is nothing to resolve and no read-only probe to check. Dorc
elides a command only when it can prove the command's effect is already in
place, so this one is left to run on every apply.
      [ book.sh:8:28
   8 |    sm_pick() { common high "$@"; }
     |                            \__/

   = repair:  Give the operand a value Dorc can resolve statically -- a literal,
              or a variable assigned from one -- so Dorc can probe it and elide
              the command once it has converged. If the value must stay dynamic,
              load an oracle that vouches for this command's convergence, and
              Dorc will guard the command instead of running it every time.
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 5 sites run unprobed (no read-only check
could be shipped): `. "$SM_ORACLE_ROOT/entry.dorc.sh"`, `package_step ambient`,
`. "$SM_ORACLE_ROOT/entry.dorc.sh"`, `package_step regional`, `package_step
after` -- run `dorc why` for the per-site detail (the apply runs each anyway, to
stay safe)
      [ book.sh:5:1
   5 | . "$SM_ORACLE_ROOT/entry.dorc.sh"
     | \_______________________________/

   = help:  sm site runs `. "$SM_ORACLE_ROOT/entry.dorc.sh"`
why: ran because operand 2 is a command-substitution `$(...)` or runtime-dynamic value -- its value couldn't be resolved (first seen at 8:16 `common high "$@"`); so dorc runs it, to stay safe (when unsure, run). to skip it, make the operand a literal Dorc can resolve+probe [resolve-dynamism]
hint: '.' (line 5) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged; 4 more unmodeled walls -- dorc why
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
